# What Arch calls the things profiles ask for.
#
# A fourth vocabulary. These are reachable through a container rather
# than by installing them on the base system, which is what makes
# "packages from another distribution" possible without mixing
# binaries in one root filesystem.
#
# Arch names are often the upstream project name unchanged, so many
# entries here look like the Debian ones. Where they differ they
# differ sharply -- base-devel rather than build-essential, and no
# lib prefix on anything.
#
# A capability with no entry here loads its packages and offers none
# of them, which looks exactly like the source being empty. That is
# the maintenance cost of the alias layer, and it is the reason to
# keep the capability set small and shared rather than per-profile.
#
# Arch repositories layer, and they have to be loaded together:
#
#     core.db        the base system
#     extra.db       applications, including metasploit and ghidra
#     blackarch.db   a security overlay, ~5,000 tools
#
# BlackArch is an overlay rather than a distribution: its packages
# depend on Arch's, so loading it alone gives tools whose
# dependencies cannot resolve. Loading one database and finding
# "cannot be used: git" means a lower layer is missing, not that the
# package is broken.

Capability: web-browser
Resolves-To: firefox | chromium | epiphany | falkon

Capability: terminal-emulator
Resolves-To: foot | alacritty | kitty | konsole | gnome-terminal | xterm

Capability: file-manager
Resolves-To: dolphin | nautilus | thunar | pcmanfm

